  "extractionCalcLearnWhatIsEyTitle": "What is extraction yield?",
  "@extractionCalcLearnWhatIsEyTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the first Learn section on the brew calculator screen."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnWhatIsEyBody": "Of the ground coffee in your brewer, only part dissolves into the cup. Extraction yield is that part as a percentage. Around 18–22% usually tastes balanced: much lower reads sour and weak-sweet (under-extracted), much higher reads bitter and drying (over-extracted). It's a guide, not a law — trust your taste first.",
  "@extractionCalcLearnWhatIsEyBody": {
    "description": "Body text of the first Learn section explaining extraction yield."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnTdsTitle": "What is TDS and how do I measure it?",
  "@extractionCalcLearnTdsTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the second Learn section on the brew calculator screen."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnTdsBody": "Total dissolved solids: how much coffee is actually in the liquid — the strength of the cup. You measure it with a coffee refractometer: let a small sample cool for a minute, zero the device with distilled water, then read a few drops of filtered brew. Filter coffee usually lands at 1.15–1.45%, espresso at 8–12%. No refractometer? This calculator can't estimate TDS — but the rest of the app never needs one.",
  "@extractionCalcLearnTdsBody": {
    "description": "Body text of the second Learn section explaining TDS and how to measure it."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnStrengthVsExtractionTitle": "Strength vs extraction — not the same thing",
  "@extractionCalcLearnStrengthVsExtractionTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the third Learn section on the brew calculator screen."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnStrengthVsExtractionBody": "Strength is how concentrated the drink is (TDS); extraction is how much you took from the grounds (EY). You can brew a strong but under-extracted cup and a weak but over-extracted one. Grind and time mostly move extraction; dose and water mostly move strength.",
  "@extractionCalcLearnStrengthVsExtractionBody": {
    "description": "Body text of the third Learn section explaining strength vs extraction."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnBeverageEstimateTitle": "Why we estimate beverage weight",
  "@extractionCalcLearnBeverageEstimateTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the fourth Learn section, shown only in filter mode."
  },
  "extractionCalcLearnBeverageEstimateBody": "Grounds hold on to roughly twice their weight in water, so the cup weighs less than what you poured. The estimate subtracts 2 g per gram of coffee. Weighing the actual cup (server on a scale, tare first) is always more accurate.",
  "@extractionCalcLearnBeverageEstimateBody": {
    "description": "Body text of the fourth Learn section explaining the beverage weight estimate, shown only in filter mode."
  },
